{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfjC6qoLXapdWtFB1HMAP","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01XeZkrE85ciVKW5tbBLUuZq","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"D=/scratch/users/kushinm/auto-psych/outer_loop_live/run3/data/subjective_randomness/experiment2/model_loop/iter_4/candidate_0\ncat << 'EOF' > $D/hypothesis.md\nPeople judge a sequence random to the extent their own model of a fair coin explains it better than the regular ways a sequence could have been made — and among those regular ways they entertain a kind no current model considers: a sequence built by symmetry, whose second half is the first half mirrored (a palindrome, HHTTTTHH), mirrored with the faces swapped (HHTHTHTT), or repeated with the faces swapped (HHTTTTHH… e.g. HTTH THHT), copied with occasional slips. So sequences that are globally symmetric look designed and less random, even when their switch count, heads/tails balance and short periodic motifs are unremarkable (everything else as in the current best account: a person-specific believed switch rate and second-order switch belief, a switch-biased coin, a biased coin, a repeating short motif with each person's own motif suspicion, evidence weighed per flip, person-specific side habit).\nEOF\necho symmetry_generator_suspicion > $D/model_name.txt\nsed -n '1,0p' /dev/null\ncd /scratch/users/kushinm/auto-psych/outer_loop_live/run3/data/subjective_randomness/experiment2/model_loop\n/scratch/users/kushinm/auto-psych/outer_loop_live/venv/bin/python - << 'PY'\nsrc=open('models/second_order_motif_side_habit.py').read()\nD='iter_4/candidate_0/candidate.py'\ndoc_end=src.index('\"\"\"',3)+3\nnewdoc='''\"\"\"Symmetry-built sequences as a suspected regular generator.\n\nPeople judge a sequence random to the extent their own model of a fair coin\nexplains it better than the regular generators. The single new claim: among the\nregular generators people also entertain a *symmetry* generator, which writes the\nfirst half of a sequence freely and builds the second half from it by one of\nthree transformations -- mirror (palindrome), mirror with faces swapped, or\nrepeat with faces swapped -- copied with occasional slips (fitted slip rate),\nwith a fitted shared prior weight exp(s) relative to the other regular\ngenerators. Globally symmetric sequences therefore look designed.\n\nEverything else is the framework of `second_order_motif_side_habit`: a\nperson-specific believed switch rate and shared second-order switch belief for\nchance; a switch-biased Markov coin, a biased coin and a repeating short motif\n(person-specific motif weight) as regular generators; per-flip evidence scaling\n(n / 5) ** -gamma; person-specific sensitivity and side habit.\n\"\"\"'''\nsrc=newdoc+src[doc_end:]\n# features\nsrc=src.replace('''    out[\"n\"] = float(n)\n    return out''','''    half = n // 2\n    for name, pairs in (\n        (\"mir\", [(seq[i], seq[n - 1 - i], False) for i in range(half)]),\n        (\"cmir\", [(seq[i], seq[n - 1 - i], True) for i in range(half)]),\n        (\"crep\", [(seq[i], seq[i + n - half], True) for i in range(half)]),\n    ):\n        out[f\"s_{name}\"] = float(sum(1 for x, y, flip in pairs if (x != y) != flip))\n    out[\"half\"] = float(half)\n    out[\"n\"] = float(n)\n    return out''')\nsrc=src.replace('''        \"seq_len\": np.asarray([t[\"n\"] for t in table], dtype=\"float64\"),\n    }''','''        \"seq_len\": np.asarray([t[\"n\"] for t in table], dtype=\"float64\"),\n        \"seq_half\": np.asarray([t[\"half\"] for t in table], dtype=\"float64\"),\n    }\n    for s in SYMS:\n        out[f\"seq_sym_{s}\"] = np.asarray([t[f\"s_{s}\"] for t in table], dtype=\"float64\")''')\nsrc=src.replace('PERIODS = (1, 2, 3, 4)\\n','PERIODS = (1, 2, 3, 4)\\nSYMS = (\"mir\", \"cmir\", \"crep\")\\n')\nsrc=src.replace('''    seq_mis = {p: pm.Data(f\"seq_mis{p}\", np.zeros(1, dtype=\"float64\")) for p in PERIODS}\n''','''    seq_mis = {p: pm.Data(f\"seq_mis{p}\", np.zeros(1, dtype=\"float64\")) for p in PERIODS}\n    seq_half = pm.Data(\"seq_half\", np.ones(1, dtype=\"float64\"))\n    seq_sym = {s: pm.Data(f\"seq_sym_{s}\", np.zeros(1, dtype=\"float64\")) for s in SYMS}\n''')\nsrc=src.replace('''    # Person-specific prior weight of the motif generator''','''    # Symmetry generator: first half free (n - half flips), second half copied\n    # from it by a transformation with slip rate eps_sym; averaged over the\n    # three transformations; shared prior weight exp(s_sym).\n    logit_eps_sym = pm.Normal(\"logit_eps_sym\", mu=-2.0, sigma=1.0)\n    eps_sym = pm.math.sigmoid(logit_eps_sym)\n    sym_terms = [\n        -(seq_len - seq_half) * math.log(2.0)\n        + (seq_half - seq_sym[s]) * pt.log1p(-eps_sym)\n        + seq_sym[s] * pt.log(eps_sym)\n        for s in SYMS\n    ]\n    s_sym = pm.Normal(\"s_sym\", mu=-1.0, sigma=1.5)\n    log_sym = (\n        pm.math.logsumexp(pt.stack(sym_terms, axis=0), axis=0, keepdims=False)\n        - math.log(len(SYMS))\n        + s_sym\n    )\n\n    # Person-specific prior weight of the motif generator''')\nsrc=src.replace('''            pt.stack([seq_lmark[idx], seq_lbias[idx], log_motif[idx] + a_motif], axis=0),''','''            pt.stack(\n                [seq_lmark[idx], seq_lbias[idx], log_motif[idx] + a_motif, pt.broadcast_to(log_sym[idx], a_motif.shape)],\n                axis=0,\n            ),''')\nopen(D,'w').write(src)\nPY\ncd /scratch/users/kushinm/auto-psych/outer_loop_live/runs/run3/repo\n/scratch/users/kushinm/auto-psych/outer_loop_live/venv/bin/python -c \"\nimport sys;sys.path.insert(0,'$D');import importlib.util as u\ns=u.spec_from_file_location('c','$D/candidate.py');m=u.module_from_spec(s);s.loader.exec_module(m)\nfor q in ['HHTTTTHH','HHTHTHTT','HTTHTHHT','HTHHTHTT']: print(q,{k:v for k,v in m._summary(q).items() if k.startswith('s_')})\""},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage"